<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=abacus">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abacus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2700–2300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/28th-century-bce">28th century BCE</a> · Also known as Counting frame</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An abacus, or counting frame, is a hand-operated calculating tool of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> or counters moved on a board or rods, first known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, where it appeared between 2700 and 2300 BCE with columns for the orders of magnitude of the base-60 number system. The marble Salamis Tablet of 300 BCE is the oldest counting board yet discovered. Greek, Roman and Chinese forms followed, and abacuses remained in everyday use until handheld electronic calculators largely replaced them in the 1980s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abacus.jpg" alt="Abacus" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Tor Svensson · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kulram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>2700–2300 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Sumer</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The form of the Sumerian device is unknown; the oldest surviving counting board, the Salamis Tablet, dates from about 300 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sexagesimal">Base-60 numbers<span>3rd millennium BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=abacus">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
